<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ruleset name="Groundwork Common Post Portal">

	<description>
		WordPress plus WordPress-Docs, which is what a WordPress.org reviewer
		runs — so the same output is available here before they see it. The
		codebase satisfies it: `composer lint` is clean.

		── Why the full standard and not WordPress-Extra ────────────────────

		This read `WordPress-Extra` for most of its life, on the assumption that
		it was the same thing. It is not. Two sniffs live only in the full
		`WordPress` standard: WordPress.DB.SlowDBQuery, and
		WordPress.Security.ValidatedSanitizedInput — which is the sniff that
		catches superglobal input reaching code without being sanitized.

		Of all three Groundwork Common plugins this is the one that could least
		afford to have it switched off. This is the plugin with public front-end
		form handling, file uploads out of the webroot, and sign-in links where
		possession of the link *is* the authentication.

		Switching it on reported five findings, all ValidatedSanitizedInput and
		none of them exploitable — but three of the five were lines that already
		carried a phpcs:ignore naming this exact sniff. Those annotations had
		never suppressed anything, so they had never been tested, and every one
		of them was on the wrong line: `phpcs:ignore` covers its own line and the
		next, and each violation was reported on the *third* line of a multi-line
		ternary. They now sit on the line they excuse. See the note in
		inc/auth.php for the one that got a code change instead.

		It also armed twelve annotations that were decoration until now — the
		SlowDBQuery ones in access.php, auth.php, changeset.php, field-media.php,
		meta-box.php and org-cpt.php, plus the ValidatedSanitizedInput ones in
		field-media.php and portal.php, which is to say the upload path and the
		front-end POST dispatcher.

		── On the annotations ───────────────────────────────────────────────

		Getting green meant taking the sniffs seriously rather than switching off
		the ones that complained. The first pass reported 503 findings and it was
		tempting to silence half a dozen rules wholesale; that produces a green
		run which proves nothing. Instead the formatting was fixed (268 of them
		by phpcbf), the block comments were reflowed, and the rules that really
		are wrong for one line each carry a phpcs:ignore *on that line* saying
		why. There are around fifty-five of those, each with a written
		justification, and they are reviewable in a way a ruleset-wide severity 0
		is not.

		Fifteen more were deleted, having been checked one at a time by
		neutralising each and re-linting: they suppressed nothing. Twelve sat on
		nonce lines explaining that the nonce is verified just below, which WPCS
		already understands; three sat on $query->set( 'meta_query', ... ) calls,
		which SlowDBQuery does not read because it only inspects array literals.
		Their prose was kept as ordinary comments. A stale ignore on a security
		line is not inert — it suppresses nothing today, but it reads as "a sniff
		is watching this", and it would silence the warning for real if the check
		above it were ever moved or removed.

		Exactly one rule is off wholesale, at the bottom, with its reason.
	</description>

	<!--
		The shipped plugin, and only that. tests/ is in .distignore, never
		reaches a user or a reviewer, and is written against a different set of
		constraints — its whole job is to declare functions named after core's
		and echo "PASS" to a terminal, both of which this standard rightly
		objects to in a plugin and wrongly objects to in a test.
	-->
	<file>.</file>

	<exclude-pattern>/vendor/</exclude-pattern>
	<exclude-pattern>/node_modules/</exclude-pattern>
	<exclude-pattern>/languages/</exclude-pattern>
	<exclude-pattern>/.wordpress-org/</exclude-pattern>
	<exclude-pattern>/tests/</exclude-pattern>

	<!--
		.dev/ holds the seed and the local mu-plugins, and it is excluded for the
		same reason /tests/ above it is: the standard objects, correctly, to
		things that are wrong in a plugin and right in a fixture. The findings it
		reported were 25 unprefixed script variables, ten echoes to a terminal
		and eight deliberate global overrides — a seed script described exactly.

		The sibling volunteer tracker keeps the same artifact at tests/seed.php,
		where /tests/ already covered it. This one lives in .dev/ and so had been
		linted as though it were the plugin.

		Gitignored and in .distignore, so it reaches neither a release nor CI;
		this only affects a local run.
	-->
	<exclude-pattern>/.dev/</exclude-pattern>

	<!--
		Claude Code keeps its worktrees under .claude/, and each one is a whole
		second copy of this plugin. Without this, `composer lint` from the repo
		root reports every stale copy alongside the real source — Post Portal
		once showed 1,854 findings this way, all of them phantom. CI never sees
		it, because a fresh checkout has no worktrees, so the local run looks
		catastrophic while main is green.
	-->
	<exclude-pattern>/.claude/</exclude-pattern>

	<!--
		`edit.asset.php` is the filename @wordpress/scripts generates and the one
		register_block_type() looks for. Not ours to rename.
	-->
	<rule ref="WordPress.Files.FileName.InvalidClassFileName">
		<exclude-pattern>*.asset.php</exclude-pattern>
	</rule>
	<rule ref="WordPress.Files.FileName.NotHyphenatedLowercase">
		<exclude-pattern>*.asset.php</exclude-pattern>
	</rule>

	<arg name="extensions" value="php"/>
	<arg name="colors"/>
	<arg name="basepath" value="."/>
	<arg name="parallel" value="8"/>
	<arg value="ps"/>

	<rule ref="WordPress"/>
	<rule ref="WordPress-Docs"/>

	<!--
		Everything the sniffs need to know about this plugin, declared once so
		the prefix, domain and version rules actually have something to check
		against rather than passing vacuously.
	-->
	<config name="minimum_wp_version" value="6.3"/>
	<config name="testVersion" value="7.4-"/>

	<rule ref="WordPress.NamingConventions.PrefixAllGlobals">
		<properties>
			<property name="prefixes" type="array">
				<element value="gwc_pp"/>
				<element value="GWC_PP"/>
			</property>
		</properties>
	</rule>

	<rule ref="WordPress.WP.I18n">
		<properties>
			<property name="text_domain" type="array">
				<element value="groundwork-common-post-portal"/>
			</property>
		</properties>
	</rule>

	<!--
		── The only rule switched off wholesale ─────────────────────────────

		Callback parameters this plugin does not use but cannot rename or drop,
		because the signature belongs to the hook or to the field-type contract
		rather than to us. The plugin `unset()`s them where it reads better, but
		a sanitize callback that ignores its $field is not a defect — it is a
		callback honouring a contract.

		Every other WPCS rule is on, and the codebase satisfies it. Where a rule
		is genuinely wrong for one line, that line carries an annotated
		phpcs:ignore saying so, which is reviewable in a way a silent
		ruleset-wide severity 0 is not.
	-->
	<rule ref="Generic.CodeAnalysis.UnusedFunctionParameter">
		<severity>0</severity>
	</rule>

</ruleset>
